# The server's settings and secrets. Copy to certificates/server.env, beside the laptop's dev.env, and
# fill in. certificates/ is gitignored and must stay that way: this is a credential file.
#
#   cp deploy/server.env.example certificates/server.env
#   chmod 600 certificates/server.env
#
# Read by `deploy/compose.sh` (every compose command), and by the health and backup timers. One
# `KEY=value` a line. Put a value with `&`, `$`, `#` or spaces in single quotes ('…'), which all three
# readers take literally: an Atlas address has `&`, and a password may have `$`.

# The MongoDB Atlas connection string, password included (ROADMAP Phase 9): Atlas → Database →
# Connect → Drivers, or the one in the laptop's certificates/mongodb.env. Single-quoted, for its `&`. No
# default on purpose: compose.yml uses `${MONGODB_URI:?}`, so the stack refuses to start rather than
# coming up pointed at nothing. Allow this server's public address in Atlas → Network Access, and
# nothing wider.
MONGODB_URI=''
# The database inside the cluster. Optional; `mahoraga` when unset.
MONGODB_DB=mahoraga

# The key-encryption key for every user credential (TENANT_SAFETY.md §2). Generate with:
#
#   python -c "from libs.secrets import generate_key; print(generate_key())"
#
# Format is one or more comma-separated `<key_id>:<base64url>` entries, newest first. A bare key
# with no id is accepted and treated as `k0`, so the first deployment does not have to understand
# rotation — but naming it now is what makes the first rotation a config change:
#
#   SECRETS_KEK=k2:<new key>,k1:<retired key>
#
# New DEKs are wrapped with the FIRST entry; the rest exist only to unwrap rows written before the
# rotation. Once `SecretStore.users_needing_rewrap()` returns empty, the retired entry can be
# dropped. Removing it too early makes those users' credentials unreadable, so check first.
#
# There is deliberately no default. A service with no KEK refuses to start rather than falling back
# to something weaker, because a fallback would make every test of this design pass and the design
# itself worthless.
SECRETS_KEK=

# ⚠ Back this file up somewhere OTHER than wherever the database backups live.
# OPERATIONS_PLAN.md §7 names the two silent failure modes, and they apply to every secret the
# platform holds — but they are sharpest for SECRETS_KEK, which is the one key the whole envelope
# design assumes a database thief does not have:
#   secret stored WITH the backup  → one stolen file is a complete breach
#   secret stored NOWHERE          → the restore produces a database nobody can read
#
# Both halves belong in the restore drill: restore the database, restore the KEK from its separate
# location, and prove a credential decrypts. A drill that only restores the database proves nothing
# about whether the accounts still work.

# ── the API service ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Signs the 15-minute access token. Generate with:
#
#   python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
#
# No default, deliberately: a signing key with a known value is not a signing key, and every test
# of the auth flow would still pass with one.
ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET=

# Who sees Admin: comma-separated addresses, each an account that has signed up. Can also be granted
# per account in the database (`users.is_admin`).
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# Where verification links point. Must be the address a user's browser can actually reach — the
# Cloudflare Tunnel hostname (D7), not the VPS. Telegram's "Make my CV" buttons and browser
# notifications link here too, so the notifier reads it as well.
PUBLIC_URL=https://jobs.example.com

# ── alerts: Telegram, browser notifications and email ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Telegram needs nothing here: each person makes their own bot with @BotFather and pastes its token
# in Settings (owner's decision, 2026-11-01). The notifier decrypts it with SECRETS_KEK to send.
# The daily email of new jobs uses the SMTP settings below.

# Browser notifications (Web Push): one key pair for the platform, generated once with
#
#   python3 -c "import base64; from cryptography.hazmat.primitives import serialization as s; from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import ec; k = ec.generate_private_key(ec.SECP256R1()); b = lambda raw: base64.urlsafe_b64encode(raw).rstrip(b'=').decode(); print('VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=' + b(k.private_numbers().private_value.to_bytes(32, 'big'))); print('VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=' + b(k.public_key().public_bytes(s.Encoding.X962, s.PublicFormat.UncompressedPoint)))"
#
# **Generate it once.** A new pair strands every browser that subscribed with the old one. The API
# serves the public half; only the notifier holds the private one. VAPID_SUBJECT is how a push
# service reaches the operator about abuse: a mailto: or https: address.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:ops@example.com

# ── transactional email (Zoho, no-reply@flow-solutions.xyz) ───────────────────────────────────
#
# The platform sends signup confirmations, health alerts and delivery-failure notices as
# **no-reply@flow-solutions.xyz**, through Zoho — the same mailbox the other FlowSolutions services
# already send through, so SPF and DKIM for the domain are correct and mail does not land in spam.
# Settings confirmed against `filip_project/flowsolutions-ai/docs/setup/smtpcheck.md`.
#
# **If the mailbox has 2FA this must be a Zoho app password, not the login password.** SMTP auth
# fails with a plain password on a 2FA account, and the error reads like a wrong password rather
# than a wrong *kind* of password — which is an hour lost to the wrong theory.
#
# To test the credential before wiring it here, that repo has a standalone checker:
#   SMTPCHECK_PASSWORD='...' go run ./cmd/smtpcheck -host smtp.zoho.com -port 587 \
#       -security starttls -username no-reply@flow-solutions.xyz
#
# **With these unset the API prints emails instead of sending them.** That is deliberate — a
# developer can run the whole signup flow and read the verification link from the log — but on the
# box it is a misconfiguration that looks like nothing is wrong: signup returns success and the
# confirmation never arrives.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.zoho.eu
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=no-reply@flow-solutions.xyz
SMTP_PASSWORD=
MAIL_FROM=no-reply@flow-solutions.xyz

# The platform's own ceiling, counted over a rolling 24 hours — not a calendar day, because that is
# how a provider meters it, and a midnight reset would call 400 at 23:00 plus 400 at 01:00 two
# compliant days. Set below the provider's own limit so the headroom absorbs a burst: being refused
# by our cap is visible and recoverable, being silently throttled by Zoho is neither.
MAIL_DAILY_CAP=400

# ── the site proxy ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One residential proxy for everyone (owner's decision, 2026-11-01), the way the marketplace app
# reaches Vinted and eBay: LinkedIn refuses a server's address, so its searches and descriptions go
# through this, with no bandwidth limit per account. Either form works:
#   http://user:password@host:port      or      user:password@host:port
# Unset, LinkedIn is skipped and the harvester's log says so.
HARVEST_PROXY=

# ── the way in: the Cloudflare Tunnel ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The token of the tunnel that serves this site: Cloudflare Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels → the
# tunnel → Configure, the long string after `--token` in its "install connector" command. The public
# hostname is set there too, with the service `http://platform-api:8000` (compose.yml says why
# `platform-api` and not `api`). Empty, cloudflared exits at once and says so.
TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# ── job boards with a free key of their own (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# General boards, every industry. Each one is skipped, and its log line says so, while its key is
# empty. Adzuna: developer.adzuna.com. Findwork: findwork.dev.
ADZUNA_APP_ID=
ADZUNA_APP_KEY=
FINDWORK_API_KEY=

# ── the platform's own AI key ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One paid key gives every account with no key of its own a small daily allowance of AI requests —
# AI-written documents now, the support chat next — `ai_platform_requests_per_day`, 20 unless you
# change it per account in the admin panel. Accounts with their own Claude, Gemini or OpenAI key use
# theirs and are not counted. Left empty, only own keys write with AI; instant documents need no
# key at all. Provider is one of `anthropic`, `gemini`, `openai`. Never a free-tier key: Google's
# unpaid tier may not be offered to users in the EEA, Switzerland or the UK, or be sent personal
# data.
PLATFORM_AI_PROVIDER=
PLATFORM_AI_KEY=
